---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.endsWith("/") ? import.meta.env.BASE_URL : import.meta.env.BASE_URL + "/";
---
<Layout title="AP Research: EdTech Effectiveness — Rahul Jain">
  <div class="wrap">
    <a class="back-link" href={base + "origins/"}>&larr; Origins</a>
    <h1 class="page-title">AP Research: EdTech Effectiveness</h1>
    <p class="page-lede">High school research paper, "EdTech in High School: An Investigation into its Effectiveness Through Student Perspectives" &middot; September 2018 &ndash; June 2019</p>
    <section>
      <p class="blurb">
        This was my first real research project, and it turned out to be the first of many times I'd end
        up asking a version of the same question: does a technology actually help people learn, or does
        it just feel more convenient? I studied whether school-issued laptops improved learning outcomes
        and found a real split in student preference — roughly <strong>two-thirds preferred digital "techbooks"</strong>
        for convenience and organization, while a third preferred paper textbooks, citing better retention
        and focus.
      </p>
      <p class="blurb">
        The interesting finding wasn't the split itself, it was why: digital tools were often <strong>chosen for
        ease rather than actual learning effectiveness</strong>, and students whose learning styles were better
        suited to physical media were being left behind by a one-size-fits-all rollout. That distinction —
        between technology that's easier and technology that's actually better for learning — is the same
        question I'm still working on, just with reinforcement-learning agents instead of laptops now.
      </p>
      <div class="abstract-quote">
        <span class="abstract-label">Full Abstract</span>
        <p>
          Quality of education is one of the most important factors in a child's success in life.
          Spending their formative years with high quality educators combined with access to the right
          educational resources and tools has the potential to <strong>transform young students' lives forever</strong>.
          There have been developments in the last two decades in the digital resources available to
          students and educators in their classrooms — particularly educational technologies, or EdTech —
          adopted with the objective of transforming education for youth all across America.
        </p>
        <p>
          The ubiquitous use of technology in classrooms is designed to help students think differently
          about the content they are learning. Different technologies have been developed for different
          subjects to help students interact with content in specialized ways — PhET simulations for
          physics, iPad use for music and special education, and applications directed at a specific
          subject have been implemented at every stage of the curriculum. To further increase access,
          many students are given school-issued technology to use at school and bring home for homework,
          so that districts can offer equal opportunities to succeed academically regardless of background
          or ability to afford it. As this usage becomes ubiquitous and mandatory, this study asks: <strong>how
          effective is educational technology, really?</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          Numerous studies provide the perspectives of researchers, administrators, teachers, and parents,
          but none was found that researched the perspective of high school students themselves — <strong>a key
          gap this study set out to fill</strong>. The method consisted of four steps: meeting district officials
          for a retrospective analysis of student performance, surveying freshmen and sophomores for a
          quantitative analysis, interviewing students personally for a qualitative analysis, and
          conducting an observational study of negative technology usage in classrooms.
        </p>
        <p>
          District administrators confirmed the district does not compile or correlate student
          performance data with technology usage. Among students, <strong>83% reported using technology in 4 or
          more classes a day</strong>, with math seeing the heaviest use and history/social studies the least. 80
          students (64.5%) preferred technology platforms ("techbooks") over paper textbooks, and 44
          (35.5%) preferred paper — but of the reasons given for preferring tech, most cited convenience
          and organization, not learning effectiveness. Among upperclassmen specifically, that preference
          reversed: <strong>53% preferred paper textbooks over techbooks</strong>. Students who preferred paper were
          confident they learned better with it and were willing to give up the convenience of digital
          tools to get that; those aligned with digital learning skewed more convenience-oriented than
          learning-oriented.
        </p>
        <p>
          The over-integration of tools like Khan Academy and Discovery was largely opposed by students,
          who expressed a preference for teacher-led instruction and more classroom stimulation — many
          felt teachers had become overly reliant on technology to teach and to prepare students for exams,
          leaving students unstimulated and with less incentive to come to class. Given this split
          consensus, the natural solution proposed was to <strong>make laptop use optional rather than required</strong> —
          the same choice already extended to teachers — so students could choose whichever medium
          actually helped them learn, rather than defaulting to whichever was more convenient.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
